export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"cadcraft","addon":"harness","name":"Harness and cabinet wiring","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"harness-start","scope":"addon:harness","title":"Harness and cabinet wiring: getting started","summary":"What the Harness and cabinet wiring add-on does in CadCraft, how to give wires an id, colour and gauge, and get the from-to list and the bill of materials.","section":"start","order":1,"keywords":["harness","wire harness","wiring","from-to","wire list","cabinet","control cabinet","DIN rail","duct","terminal","ferrule","gauge","bill of materials","add-on"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-is","text":"What it is","level":2},{"id":"your-first-wiring-list","text":"Your first wiring list","level":2},{"id":"where-lengths-come-from","text":"Where lengths come from","level":2},{"id":"files-open-anywhere","text":"Files open anywhere","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-is\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHarness and cabinet wiring is an add-on for CadCraft that comes with it and is free. It works on a schematic drawn with the Electrical add-on. Each run of wire between two connection points is one wire; you give it an id, a colour, a gauge, a type and a treatment for each end. CadCraft then makes the papers a wiring job runs on: the from-to list, the wire list with cut lengths, the connector tables, the segments of a harness drawing with the wires and the diameter of each, and a bill of materials with wire, wire ends, devices, rails and ducts.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is off until you switch it on: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHarness and cabinet wiring\u003C/span\u003E. Its tools are in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWiring\u003C/span\u003E section of the right panel, as commands (below), and, when you ask for it in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWiring\u003C/span\u003E, as a group at the end of the toolbar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-first-wiring-list\"\u003EYour first wiring list\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw the schematic with the Electrical add-on (symbols with a device tag, wires on a wire layer).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EHNUM\u003C/code\u003E. Every wire without an id gets the next one (\u003Ccode\u003EW001\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EW002\u003C/code\u003E, … — the format is in the settings).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect wires and type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EHWIRE\u003C/code\u003E. Choose a field (Colour, Gauge, Type, End 1, End 2, Length, Note, Id), type its value and press Enter for the next, or Enter again to finish. Colours are the IEC two-letter codes (\u003Ccode\u003EBK\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EBN\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ERD\u003C/code\u003E …); the gauge is a size in mm² (\u003Ccode\u003E1.5\u003C/code\u003E) or an AWG (\u003Ccode\u003E18 AWG\u003C/code\u003E). Properties shows the same fields for a selected wire.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EHFROMTO\u003C/code\u003E to print the list, and click a point to draw it as a table. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EHCUT\u003C/code\u003E is the wire list with cut lengths, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EHCONN\u003C/code\u003E the connector tables (every pin and its wire), \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EHBOM\u003C/code\u003E the bill of materials.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA net of three or more pins is wired as a chain (each wire runs from one pin to the next, top to bottom) or as a star (the top left pin to every other); choose in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWiring\u003C/span\u003E. A wire&#039;s settings are kept under its two pins, so changing the choice leaves the settings of wires that no longer exist behind and gives the new ones the defaults. The two sides of a terminal stay apart, because a field wire lands on one and a panel wire leaves the other. What you set is kept with the wire&#039;s first device and follows its tag and pin, so moving things on the sheet does not lose it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-lengths-come-from\"\u003EWhere lengths come from\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA wire has no length until one of three things gives it one:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA harness drawing.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EHHARN\u003C/code\u003E draws nodes and segments (type \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E to start a branch from a node you click). \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EHEND\u003C/code\u003E ties devices to a node (a terminal strip by its tag, \u003Ccode\u003E-X1\u003C/code\u003E). \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EHROUTE\u003C/code\u003E finds each wire&#039;s shortest way along the segments, adds the allowance at each end and the service loop (settings), and writes the number of wires and the diameter of the bundle on every segment.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe cabinet layout.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EHPANEL\u003C/code\u003E draws the back panel, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EHRAIL\u003C/code\u003E a DIN rail, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EHDUCT\u003C/code\u003E a wiring duct. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EHMOD\u003C/code\u003E puts the schematic&#039;s devices on a rail; type \u003Ccode\u003E*\u003C/code\u003E to place every one that is left, side by side. A wire between two devices in the cabinet is the distance between their centres, along the duct grid, times the slack factor, plus the allowances.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA length you type\u003C/strong\u003E with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EHWIRE\u003C/code\u003E, which wins over the other two.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe diameters are typical values for stranded wire; use the real figures from the wire you buy.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files-open-anywhere\"\u003EFiles open anywhere\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHarness drawings and cabinet parts are ordinary lines and block references in the file; with the add-on off, or in another program, they show, plot and move. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E writes the from-to list, the wire list and the bill of materials as CSV, or all the lists as one workbook.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What it is Harness and cabinet wiring is an add-on for CadCraft that comes with it and is free. It works on a schematic drawn with the Electrical add-on. Each run of wire between two connection points is one wire; you give it an id, a colour, a gauge, a type and a treatment for each end. CadCraft then makes the papers a wiring job runs on: the from-to list, the wire list with cut lengths, the connector tables, the segments of a harness drawing with the wires and the diameter of each, and a bill of materials with wire, wire ends, devices, rails and ducts. It is off until you switch it on: Settings › General › Add-ons › Harness and cabinet wiring. Its tools are in the Wiring section of the right panel, as commands (below), and, when you ask for it in Settings › CadCraft › Wiring, as a group at the end of the toolbar. Your first wiring list Draw the schematic with the Electrical add-on (symbols with a device tag, wires on a wire layer). Type HNUM. Every wire without an id gets the next one (W001, W002, … — the format is in the settings). Select wires and type HWIRE. Choose a field (Colour, Gauge, Type, End 1, End 2, Length, Note, Id), type its value and press Enter for the next, or Enter again to finish. Colours are the IEC two-letter codes (BK, BN, RD …); the gauge is a size in mm² (1.5) or an AWG (18 AWG). Properties shows the same fields for a selected wire. Type HFROMTO to print the list, and click a point to draw it as a table. HCUT is the wire list with cut lengths, HCONN the connector tables (every pin and its wire), HBOM the bill of materials. A net of three or more pins is wired as a chain (each wire runs from one pin to the next, top to bottom) or as a star (the top left pin to every other); choose in Settings › CadCraft › Wiring. A wire's settings are kept under its two pins, so changing the choice leaves the settings of wires that no longer exist behind and gives the new ones the defaults. The two sides of a terminal stay apart, because a field wire lands on one and a panel wire leaves the other. What you set is kept with the wire's first device and follows its tag and pin, so moving things on the sheet does not lose it. Where lengths come from A wire has no length until one of three things gives it one: A harness drawing. HHARN draws nodes and segments (type B to start a branch from a node you click). HEND ties devices to a node (a terminal strip by its tag, -X1). HROUTE finds each wire's shortest way along the segments, adds the allowance at each end and the service loop (settings), and writes the number of wires and the diameter of the bundle on every segment. The cabinet layout. HPANEL draws the back panel, HRAIL a DIN rail, HDUCT a wiring duct. HMOD puts the schematic's devices on a rail; type * to place every one that is left, side by side. A wire between two devices in the cabinet is the distance between their centres, along the duct grid, times the slack factor, plus the allowances. A length you type with HWIRE, which wins over the other two. The diameters are typical values for stranded wire; use the real figures from the wire you buy. Files open anywhere Harness drawings and cabinet parts are ordinary lines and block references in the file; with the add-on off, or in another program, they show, plot and move. Export writes the from-to list, the wire list and the bill of materials as CSV, or all the lists as one workbook."},{"slug":"harness-checks","scope":"addon:harness","title":"Harness and cabinet wiring: the checks","summary":"What the Harness and cabinet wiring add-on warns about in Problems, and how to fix each.","section":"reference","order":20,"keywords":["problems","checks","wire id","unrouted","overlap","rail","unplaced"],"headings":[{"id":"what-is-checked","text":"What is checked","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-checked\"\u003EWhat is checked\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe checks appear in the Problems panel and update when you change the drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWires with no id.\u003C/strong\u003E A note. Fix: \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EHNUM\u003C/code\u003E numbers them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWires with no length.\u003C/strong\u003E A warning, shown once the drawing has a harness or a cabinet device: their devices are not tied to a harness node, not both in the cabinet, and no length was typed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDevices overlapping on a rail\u003C/strong\u003E, or \u003Cstrong\u003Ea device past the end of its rail.\u003C/strong\u003E A warning. Devices count as on a rail when their centre is within the rail&#039;s width.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESchematic devices not in the cabinet yet.\u003C/strong\u003E A note, once any device is placed. Field devices (motors, lamps, switches, push buttons) are not expected in the cabinet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECabinet devices the schematic does not have.\u003C/strong\u003E A warning: the tag on the device does not match any tag in the schematic.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe module sizes (a one-pole breaker is 17.5 mm wide, a contactor 45 mm, a terminal 6.2 mm) are typical; change a device&#039;s size in Properties to match the part you buy.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What is checked The checks appear in the Problems panel and update when you change the drawing. Wires with no id. A note. Fix: HNUM numbers them. Wires with no length. A warning, shown once the drawing has a harness or a cabinet device: their devices are not tied to a harness node, not both in the cabinet, and no length was typed. Devices overlapping on a rail, or a device past the end of its rail. A warning. Devices count as on a rail when their centre is within the rail's width. Schematic devices not in the cabinet yet. A note, once any device is placed. Field devices (motors, lamps, switches, push buttons) are not expected in the cabinet. Cabinet devices the schematic does not have. A warning: the tag on the device does not match any tag in the schematic. The module sizes (a one-pole breaker is 17.5 mm wide, a contactor 45 mm, a terminal 6.2 mm) are typical; change a device's size in Properties to match the part you buy."},{"slug":"harness-not-built-yet","scope":"addon:harness","title":"Harness and cabinet wiring: not built yet","summary":"What the Harness and cabinet wiring add-on does not do yet, so you know before you plan a job around it.","section":"reference","order":90,"keywords":["limits","not built","missing","nailboard","formboard","duct fill","connector library","roadmap"],"headings":[{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA nailboard drawing at full size.\u003C/strong\u003E The harness drawing is a plan of nodes and segments with their lengths, not a plotted board with connector images.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA connector and terminal library.\u003C/strong\u003E Devices come from the schematic&#039;s symbols; the wire end is a choice from a short list (ferrule, ring, fork …), not a part number.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDuct fill.\u003C/strong\u003E The ducts are drawn and listed, but how full they are is not calculated.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERouting in 3D.\u003C/strong\u003E Lengths in the cabinet are the distance between centres along the duct grid, times a slack factor you set; wires are not laid out one by one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWires between two sheets of a cable.\u003C/strong\u003E A multi-core cable is listed as separate wires; cable markers from the Electrical add-on are not read.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Not built yet A nailboard drawing at full size. The harness drawing is a plan of nodes and segments with their lengths, not a plotted board with connector images. A connector and terminal library. Devices come from the schematic's symbols; the wire end is a choice from a short list (ferrule, ring, fork …), not a part number. Duct fill. The ducts are drawn and listed, but how full they are is not calculated. Routing in 3D. Lengths in the cabinet are the distance between centres along the duct grid, times a slack factor you set; wires are not laid out one by one. Wires between two sheets of a cable. A multi-core cable is listed as separate wires; cable markers from the Electrical add-on are not read."}]};
